  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Back in the kitchen, the Chef finally shows Maks the real, unglamorous ingredients of the famous beef pâté, and Maks is completely shocked by what he sees.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Паштет говяжий.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Гадость страшная.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Но если всё смешать...",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Ей нравится.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "maks",
      "t": "Вы издеваетесь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Почему?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Ты самовоспитывался.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Ну, пойдём.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Кстати, хороший паштет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Как делаешь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Что добавлял?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "maks",
      "t": "Говядина, печень.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let's review the scene line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Паштет говяжий.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Beef pâté.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'pâté' straight to 'beef'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Гадость страшная.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Absolutely revolting.' Chef Viktor combines 'a disgusting thing' with 'terrible' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Но если всё смешать...",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But when you mix it all together...' Chef Viktor links 'but' directly with 'if' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Ей нравится.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She likes it.' Chef Viktor combines 'to her' with 'is pleasing' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "maks",
      "t": "Вы издеваетесь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you kidding me?' Max combines 'you' with 'are you mocking' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Почему?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why?' Chef Viktor asks this with a single word meaning 'why', using rising intonation to turn it into a quick question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Ты самовоспитывался.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You were learning to discipline yourself.' Chef Viktor combines 'you' with 'educate oneself' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Ну, пойдём.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, let's go.' In Russian, 'let's go' leads into 'let's go' in a very natural everyday phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Кстати, хороший паштет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'By the way, it's good pâté.' Chef Viktor combines 'by the way' with 'pâté' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Как делаешь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'How do you make it?' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'how' with 'are you doing'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Что добавлял?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What did you put in?' Chef Viktor links 'what' directly with 'to add' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "maks",
      "t": "Говядина, печень.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Beef, liver.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'beef' straight to 'liver'. Let's listen to the dialog one more time.",
      "gap": 0.9
